PanVK (CSF): let a draw kill the fragments of earlier draws (forward pixel kill, FPK) although it leaves a bound color attachment unwritten.

The rule so far was: a draw may kill only if it writes every bound color attachment. CoreAnimation's passes bind a memoryless, write-masked second color
attachment next to the one they fill, so none of their opaque full-screen fills could kill the fills before it: all of them were shaded (4 or 5 per pass,
about 1.6 ms each at 750 MHz; a heavy pass took 7.8 ms, of which 7.2 were these fills).

The condition is not needed on a Mali-G610: a fragment kills an earlier one only when its draw writes at least the attachments the earlier fragment's draw
writes (the draws' render-target masks, which the previous patch sets from what the draw really writes). Measured on the RK3588 board with a benchmark
that draws a heavy victim into one or both of two targets and four constant killers into one or both (all 9 pairs of write sets, 2 targets): a victim is
killed (the pass gets 0.5 ms cheaper than the victim alone) exactly when the killers write a superset of its set, and in the other 4 pairs the targets only
the victim writes keep every pixel of it (0 of 2041088 differ from the victim alone), with FPK allowed for the killers. In the UI the median command
buffer of a swipe dropped from 14.1 to 9.5 ms of GPU time.

PANVK_FPK=bound restores the old rule (an A/B switch; the plugin passes the boot-arg mali-panvk-fpk=bound on).

--- a/src/panfrost/vulkan/panvk_cmd_draw.h
+++ b/src/panfrost/vulkan/panvk_cmd_draw.h
@@ -10,6 +10,9 @@
 #error "PAN_ARCH must be defined"
 #endif
 
+#include <stdlib.h>
+#include <string.h>
+
 #include "panvk_blend.h"
 #include "panvk_cmd_desc_state.h"
 #include "panvk_cmd_query.h"
@@ -40,6 +43,22 @@
    struct panvk_image_view *dst_iview;
 };
 
+/* PANVK_FPK=bound: a draw may kill the fragments of earlier draws (forward pixel kill, FPK) only if it writes every
+ * color attachment that is bound, which is what this driver used to require of every draw. Without it a draw that
+ * leaves a bound attachment unwritten may kill too. On a Mali-G610 a fragment kills an earlier one only when its draw
+ * writes at least the attachments the earlier fragment's draw writes, so nothing a killed fragment wrote to an
+ * attachment the killer leaves alone is lost (measured for every pair of write sets of two attachments: a heavy draw
+ * writing attachment 1 survives four fills that write attachment 0 only, and those fills kill each other).
+ * Core Animation's passes bind a memoryless, write-masked second color attachment next to the one they fill, and with
+ * the old rule every one of their overdrawn opaque fills was shaded. */
+static inline bool
+panvk_fpk_needs_all_rts_from_env(void)
+{
+   const char *v = getenv("PANVK_FPK");
+
+   return v && !strcmp(v, "bound");
+}
+
 struct panvk_rendering_state {
    VkRenderingFlags flags;
    uint32_t layer_count;
@@ -47,6 +66,9 @@
